  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 100 AM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nThe long-term forecast has come more into focus amongst the \ndifferent model solutions, showing cooler and much more active \nweather into early next week. \n\nStarting with the broader picture, two upper lows will be moving \nonshore, one from the Pacific Northwest and one on the southern \nCalifornia coast. The lows will phase with a broad upper trough, \ndeepening and moving into our region by Sunday. Heavy rainfall will \nfirst spread over the San Juans, then to the rest of the Continental \nDivide and San Luis Valley prior to Monday. Some areas may see up to \n2 inches or so of QPF during this timeframe, and with snow levels \ninitially above 11 thousand feet, almost all of it should fall as \nrain. Flash flooding is going to be a major concern across the San \nJuans and portions of the San Luis Valley, especially considering \nhow much moisture those areas have received recently. For the peaks \nof the mountains, again mainly above 11 kft, expect periods of \nmoderate to heavy snowfall. Heaviest accumulations will be over the \nSan Juans, with almost all of the recent model guidance showing well \nover a foot by the end of the event.\n\nThe upper low will pass overhead on Monday, dragging a deepening \nsurface low over the plains and sending a cold front down behind it. \nThis will spread precipitation across the plains while also bringing \nin some stronger winds out of the north. Meanwhile, cooler air will \nlower snow levels across the region, down below 9000 feet by early \nMonday morning before the system pushes east. Winds will be the main \nconcern over the plains, with some models showing gusts up to 50 mph \npossible, along with widespread rain showers.\n\nThe system will push east of our area on Tuesday, leaving our area \nmild and dry with breezy north winds over the eastern plains, though \nless gusty than on Monday. Temperatures will remain somewhat cool \ntowards the middle of next week, with highs dropping into the 50s, \nwith breezy winds and decreasing cloud cover.",
